<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<%- include('../partials/public-head') %>
</head>
<body data-page="preview" data-slug="<%= websiteType.slug %>">
<%- include('../partials/public-body-start-scripts') %>
<div class="site-shell">
  <header class="site-header">
    <h1>Preview: <%= websiteType.name %></h1>
    <p style="margin:0.25rem 0 0; font-size:0.85rem; color:#666;">
      This is a fully interactive preview — buttons, links, and any animations in the site work normally. It's not a real deployment yet.
    </p>
  </header>
  <main class="site-main">
    <!--
      v1.1.6 Part F: sandbox-limitation disclaimer, preview.ejs ONLY --
      this view is never reused for the deployed-site output itself (that
      HTML comes from a completely separate code path — see
      lib/finalizeDeployment.js / lib/cloudflarePages.js — this page only
      ever wraps it in an iframe for interactive testing), so this notice
      has no way to leak onto a real deployed client site.

      Copy is deliberately non-technical -- no "sandbox"/"iframe"/
      "allow-same-origin" -- per the brief: reassuring, not alarming, and
      not something a non-technical client needs to parse as a warning
      about their own site being broken.

      Styled with this file's OWN existing (pre-Skilline-redesign) design
      tokens from site.css's :root -- --surface-alt/--border/--text-dim
      for the shell, --warning for a soft left-accent that reads as "a
      heads-up," not --danger (which would read as an error). Not
      hc-panel/Tailwind: this page hasn't been migrated to that newer
      design system yet, and introducing it for one banner on an
      otherwise-unmigrated page would look more inconsistent, not less.
    -->
    <div style="margin:0 0 1rem; padding:0.75rem 1rem; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-alt); border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--warning); font-size:0.85rem; color:var(--text-dim);">
      Heads up — a few small things like animations or responsive behavior might look slightly different here than they will once your site is live. Nothing to worry about — it'll work as expected after deployment.
    </div>
    <div class="preview-frame-wrap" id="previewFrameWrap">
      <!--
        v1.1.5 Part A — sandbox is "allow-scripts allow-popups allow-forms",
        deliberately WITHOUT "allow-same-origin":
          - allow-scripts: lets the generated site's own JS actually run
            (animations, interactive buttons, in-page nav) instead of the
            previous fully static/inert render.
          - allow-popups: lets a link with target="_blank" (see the new
            external-link post-processing in lib/externalLinks.js) actually
            open a new tab instead of being silently blocked.
          - allow-forms: in case a pasted template has its own <form>
            elements that submit/interact.
        allow-same-origin is excluded ON PURPOSE, not an oversight — do
        NOT add it back. Combining allow-scripts with allow-same-origin on
        the SAME iframe is a documented browser sandbox-escape footgun:
        together, they let the sandboxed document's script reach back into
        the parent page's actual origin (this page, and everything it can
        reach — including, if this were ever reused for an authenticated
        surface, session cookies). Without allow-same-origin, the iframe's
        document has an opaque, unique origin no matter what — its script
        can run freely INSIDE itself, but has no way out. The one
        real-feature cost: this parent page's own JS can no longer read or
        reach into iframe.contentDocument (cross-origin now, by design) —
        nothing in this preview flow ever needed to; see public/site.js's
        initPreviewPage() for how the watermark/copy-protection additions
        below work AROUND that constraint instead (injected into the
        iframe's own HTML string before it's set, not reached into
        afterward from outside).
      -->
      <iframe id="previewFrame" sandbox="allow-scripts allow-popups allow-forms" title="Site preview"></iframe>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem;">
      <button type="button" id="backBtn" class="btn-secondary">Back</button>
      <button type="button" id="fullscreenBtn" class="btn-secondary">View fullscreen</button>
      <a class="btn" href="/build/<%= websiteType.slug %>/checkout">Deploy this site</a>
    </p>
  </main>
</div>
<%- include('../partials/public-footer-scripts') %>
</body>
</html>
